      "source": "## Python Concepts Used in This Notebook\n\nA quick guide to the Python tools in this project that go beyond the basics. Each entry says **what it does**, **where we use it**, and **what the plain-Python version would look like**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. `@dataclass`: classes without the boilerplate\n*Used in:* `Card` (Cell 2), `HandRank` (Cell 3), `TableState` (Cell 7)\n\nWriting `@dataclass` above a class makes Python generate `__init__`, `__repr__` (how it prints) and `__eq__` (`==`) from the list of fields.\n\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True, order=True)\nclass Card:\n    rank: int   # 2..14\n    suit: str   # 'c', 'd', 'h', 's'\n```\n\n| Option | Meaning | Why we need it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `frozen=True` | The object can't be changed after it's created | Cards can go in a `set` (used to catch duplicate cards), and bots can't tamper with the `TableState` they're given |\n| `order=True` | Generates `<`, `>` etc., comparing fields in order (rank, then suit) | `sorted(cards)` works |\n| `eq=False` | \"Don't generate `==`, we write our own\" | Two hands are equal if they're equally strong, even with different cards |\n\n**Plain version:** a normal class with `__init__`, `__eq__`, `__lt__`, `__hash__` and `__repr__` written out by hand. It works the same but is about 15 lines longer.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `IntEnum`: named numbers\n*Used in:* `HandCategory` (Cell 3)\n\n`HandCategory.FLUSH` **is** the number `5`, so categories compare like numbers (`FLUSH > STRAIGHT`), but they print as names and can't be mistyped.\n\n**Plain version:** constants such as `HIGH_CARD, ONE_PAIR, ... = 0, 1, ...` plus a list of names for printing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `@total_ordering`: write two comparisons, get all of them\n*Used in:* `HandRank` (Cell 3)\n\nWe write `__eq__` and `__lt__`; it fills in `<=`, `>` and `>=`. That lets us use `max()` to pick the best hand and `<` in the tests.\n\n**Plain version:** write all four comparison methods yourself.\n\n---\n\n### 4. `@property`, `@staticmethod`, `@classmethod`\n*Used in:* several cells\n\n| Decorator | What it does | Example in our code |\n|---|---|---|\n| `@property` | A method you read like a variable (no parentheses) | `engine.pot` is recalculated from the players' bets every time, so it can never be out of date |\n| `@staticmethod` | A function stored inside a class that doesn't use `self` | `HandEvaluator.evaluate_five(cards)` |\n| `@classmethod` | Receives the class (`cls`) instead of an object, often used to build new objects | `Card.from_str(\"Ah\")` |\n\n**Plain version:** use `def pot(self)` and call `engine.pot()`, or write ordinary functions outside the class.\n\n---\n\n### 5. `Counter`: counting things\n*Used in:* the hand evaluator (Cell 3) and session stats (Cell 11)\n\n```python\nCounter([13, 13, 7, 7, 14])   # -> {13: 2, 7: 2, 14: 1}  (two pair: Kings and Sevens)\n```\n\n**Plain version:**\n```python\ncounts = {}\nfor rank in ranks:\n    counts[rank] = counts.get(rank, 0) + 1\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. `itertools.combinations`: every possible group\n*Used in:* `HandEvaluator.best_hand` (Cell 3)\n\n`combinations(seven_cards, 5)` produces all **21** five-card hands you can make from 7 cards. We score each one and keep the best.\n\n**Plain version:** five nested `for` loops. That's long and error-prone, so we keep `combinations`.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Generators (`yield`, `yield from`, `.send()`): pausing a function \u2b50\n*Used in:* `GameEngine.hand_steps` and `_betting_round` (Cell 8)\n\n**The problem:** with clickable buttons, Python can't wait for a click inside a loop the way `input()` waits for typing. The cell finishes and the click arrives later. So the engine needs to **pause in the middle of a hand** and resume later from exactly the same spot.\n\n**How it works:** a function containing `yield` pauses each time it reaches `yield`, hands a value out, and waits. `.send(x)` wakes it up, and `x` becomes the result of the `yield`.\n\n```python\ndef tiny_hand():\n    answer = yield \"Your turn: check or bet?\"   # pause #1\n    print(\"You chose\", answer)\n    yield \"Bot's turn\"                          # pause #2\n\ng = tiny_hand()\nprint(g.send(None))    # starts it    -> Your turn: check or bet?\nprint(g.send(\"bet\"))   # resumes it   -> You chose bet / Bot's turn\n```\n\nIn the engine, `action = yield player, state` means *\"here's who must act and what they can see; send me their action.\"*\n- `yield from self._betting_round(street)` lets the betting round pause the whole hand.\n- When the hand finishes, Python raises `StopIteration`, which carries the hand's record in `.value`.\n- The table UI (Cell 12) sends your button clicks in. `play_hand()` sends the bots' decisions in.\n\n**Plain version:** either play by text only with `input()`, or rewrite the engine as a \"state machine\" that stores whose turn it is, the current bet, etc., and moves one step per action. That's more code and easier to get wrong, so we keep generators.\n\n---\n\n### 8. `ipywidgets`: clickable controls in the notebook\n*Used in:* `PokerTableUI` (Cell 12)\n\n| Widget | Job |\n|---|---|\n| `Button` | Fold / Check / Call / Bet / Raise / All-in / Deal next hand |\n| `IntSlider` | Choosing the raise size |\n| `HTML` | Drawing the table and cards |\n| `Output` | Holding the showdown chart |\n| `HBox` / `VBox` | Placing widgets side by side / stacked |\n\n`button.on_click(func)` runs `func` when the button is clicked. `slider.observe(func, names=\"value\")` runs `func` when the slider moves.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Smaller syntax you'll see\n\n| Code | Meaning | Plain version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `for ... : break` followed by `else:` | The `else` runs only if the loop finished **without** `break` (here: nobody folded, so go to showdown) | A flag variable such as `folded = False` |\n| `next((a for a in items if ...), None)` | Find the first matching item, or `None` | A `for` loop with `break` |\n| `lambda _: self._act(\"fold\")` | A tiny unnamed one-line function, used for button clicks | `def on_fold(button): ...` |\n| `lambda _, f=fraction: ...` | `f=fraction` locks in this loop pass's value; without it every preset button would use the last one | A separate `def` per button |\n| `isinstance(action, tuple)` | Checks whether a bot answered `(\"raise\", 24)` or just `\"call\"` | (already basic) |\n| `share, odd_chip = divmod(pot, 2)` | Quotient and remainder at once | `pot // 2` and `pot % 2` |\n| `random.Random(seed)` | A private random generator: same seed means the same shuffle every run | `random.seed(42)` (affects all code) |\n| `html.escape(name)` | Makes text safe to put inside HTML | (could skip it, since we choose the names) |\n| `assert condition, \"message\"` | Stops with an error if the condition is false; this is how our tests work | `if not condition: raise AssertionError(\"message\")` |\n\n---\n\n### 10. Monte Carlo tools: `random.sample`, `frozenset`, a dict as a cache\n*Used in:* `calculate_equity` (Cell 5)\n\n| Code | Meaning | Plain version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `rng.sample(unseen, 7)` | Picks 7 **different** cards at random in one step (no card twice) | Shuffle a copy of the deck every time and take the top 7 (much slower) |\n| `frozenset(cards)` | A set that can't be changed, so it can be a dictionary key; order doesn't matter (`{A, K}` = `{K, A}`) | Sort the cards and use a tuple as the key |\n| `score_cache.get(key)` | Remember answers already computed: if this exact 7-card hand was scored before, reuse the score | Call the evaluator every time (correct, but slower) |"
