      "source": "### Monte Carlo Equity Engine (Teammate 1)\n\n`calculate_equity(hole_cards, community_cards, num_simulations=10000)` estimates how often a hand **wins, ties or loses** heads-up by playing out thousands of random boards and scoring each showdown with the Milestone 1 `HandEvaluator`.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Validate** the input: 2 hole cards, 0/3/4/5 board cards, no duplicate cards.\n2. **Build the unseen deck once** (52 cards minus every known card). The loop never copies or shuffles the whole deck.\n3. **Each simulation** draws, in one draw without replacement, the cards needed to complete the board to 5 (plus 2 opponent cards if the opponent is random), so cards can never collide.\n4. **Showdown:** both 7-card hands are scored with `HandEvaluator.best_hand`; the higher `HandRank.key` wins, equal keys tie.\n5. **Aggregate:** the win/tie/loss counts become percentages: `{\"win\": float, \"tie\": float, \"loss\": float}`.\n\n**Why Monte Carlo?** Pre-flop there are about 2 billion possible opponent-hand + board combinations, too many to check one by one, so we sample 10,000. The result is an *estimate*, usually within about \u00b11 percentage point, so two runs can differ slightly. Pass `seed=` to get identical numbers every run.\n\n**Optional extras** (the required signature still works unchanged):\n- `opponent_cards=None`: `None` = opponent holds 2 random unseen cards (re-dealt each simulation); or pass their 2 known cards.\n- `seed=None`: makes results reproducible (uses a private `random.Random(seed)`).\n\n**Performance:** a score cache remembers the evaluator's result for any 7-card hand already seen. On the flop your hand can only come out about 1,000 ways, so most evaluations become instant lookups."
